  "task_completions": [
    "The first airline meal served during a flight was on **September 11, 1919**, by **Handley Page Transport** (a British airline). \n\nHere's the story:\n\n*   **The Flight:** It was a flight from London to Paris.\n*   **The Meal:** Passengers were served a simple, pre-packaged meal of sandwiches, fruits, biscuits, and tea. It was served in wicker baskets!\n*   **The Reason:** The airline realized that the flights were long enough (around 2 hours) that passengers could get hungry and it would be a good way to improve the passenger experience.\n\nWhile earlier flights had *some* refreshments, this is considered the first instance of a full, planned meal service *during* a flight, not just before or after.",
    "The first airline meal served during a flight was on **September 11, 1919**, on a flight from London to Paris operated by **Handley Page Transport**. \n\nHere's the breakdown:\n\n* **Airline:** Handley Page Transport (considered the world's first scheduled airline)\n* **Flight:** London to Paris\n* **Meal:** A simple box lunch consisting of sandwiches, fruits, and chocolates. It cost 3 shillings (about $1.50 today).\n* **Why it happened:** The flight was quite long for the time (around 2 hours and 30 minutes) and the airline realized passengers would appreciate a refreshment.\n\n\n\nWhile earlier flights may have had passengers bring their own food, this is considered the first instance of an airline *providing* a meal as part of the service. It was a luxury at the time and aimed at attracting first-class passengers.",
    "The first airline meal served during a flight was on **September 11, 1919**, on a flight between London and Paris operated by **Handley Page Transport**. \n\nHere's the story:\n\n*   **Handley Page Transport** was a British airline that pioneered scheduled air services.\n*   They served a **pre-packaged meal of sandwiches, fruits, biscuits, and tea** to passengers on this particular route.\n*   The meals were quite rudimentary by today's standards, but it was a groundbreaking step towards passenger comfort and service in the early days of commercial aviation.\n\nIt's worth noting that while this was the first *served* meal, passengers had brought their own food on earlier flights. This was the first instance of the airline *providing* sustenance."
  ],
